  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Past ('yakatakura')",
      "content": "The verb 'yakatakura' expresses a completed past action. Shona has two main ways to talk about the past: 1) For actions that happened earlier today or very recently, combine the short subject prefix ('nda-' = I, 'wa-' = you, 'a-' = he/she, 'ta-' = we, 'va-' = they) directly with the verb stem (e.g., 'ndamuka' = 'I woke up today', 'wabudirira' = 'you have succeeded'). 2) For events that happened yesterday or further in the past, insert '-ka-' after the subject prefix (e.g., 'takatenga' = 'we bought', 'vakaenda' = 'they went', 'akadanidzira' = 'he shouted').",
      "rule": "Recent Today Past: [nda- / wa- / ta- / va-] + verb | Earlier Past: [ndaka- / waka- / taka- / vaka-] + verb"
    },
    {
      "title": "Actions Happening Right Now: The '-ri ku-' Progressive",
      "content": "To describe an action that is happening right now ('I am doing', 'it is raining', 'they are walking'), Shona uses the helper verb '-ri' ('am/is/are') followed by 'ku-' ('to') and the action verb. For people, the forms are 'ndiri ku-' (I am), 'uri ku-' (you are), 'ari ku-' (he/she is), 'tiri ku-' (we are), and 'vari ku-' (they are). In fast speech or writing, the two words are sometimes joined together (e.g., 'ndiri kuenda' or 'ndirikuenda').",
      "rule": "[Subject + -ri] + ku-[verb] -> Ndiri kuverenga (I am reading) | Vari kufamba (They are walking) | Mvura iri kunaya (It is raining)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Colours & Describing Objects in Shona ('chena')",
      "content": "This passage uses colour vocabulary such as 'chena'. Shona has three traditional core colour adjectives that change their prefix slightly to match the noun they describe: '-chena' (white/clean, e.g., 'ruvara ruchena' or 'shangu chena'), '-tema / nhema' (black/dark, e.g., 'mota nhema' or 'ruvara rutema'), and '-tsvuku' (red/bright brown, e.g., 'mota tsvuku' or 'ruvara rutsvuku'). For other colours like green ('girinhi'), blue ('bhuruu'), or orange ('orenji'), speakers typically say 'ruvara rwe-' ('the colour of...') followed by the colour name, as in 'ruvara rwegirinhi' (green).",
      "rule": "Core Colours: -chena (white), -tema / nhema (black), -tsvuku (red) | Phrase pattern: ruvara rwe-[colour] (e.g., ruvara rwegirinhi)"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Getting Around: Kombis, Buses, Rural Carts & 'Robots'",
      "content": "Daily transport in Zimbabwe features unique local vocabulary! In towns and cities, most commuters travel by white commuter minibuses universally called 'makombi' (singular: 'kombi'), while long-distance intercity trips use large buses ('bhazi') and heavy haulage trucks are affectionately nicknamed 'magonyeti'. In rural areas, donkey-drawn carts ('ngoro yemadhongi') are widely used to carry goods. And if someone tells you to turn at the 'robhoti' (plural: 'marobhotsi'), they don't mean a machine person—in Zimbabwe and Southern Africa, traffic lights are called 'robots'!",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
